1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,309
In Records, the New Record form has
Training selected in Definition.

2
00:00:04,780 --> 00:00:08,909
This built-in form provides course
information, a narrative, participant

3
00:00:08,909 --> 00:00:11,010
rows and optional unit times.

4
00:00:11,430 --> 00:00:15,420
Our fictional Chicago Fire exercise
demonstrates the record lifecycle.

5
00:00:15,770 --> 00:00:19,209
It does not represent a physical training
session or attendance.

6
00:00:19,659 --> 00:00:23,899
Enter a distinct Course, Training Only,
Records Software Entry,

7
00:00:24,059 --> 00:00:25,620
followed by the exercise date.

8
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:30,139
The Course code is Academy Rec
Twenty-Nine, and External ID carries

9
00:00:30,139 --> 00:00:31,870
a separate exercise reference.

10
00:00:32,259 --> 00:00:34,840
Location says Fictional Software
Workspace.

11
00:00:35,270 --> 00:00:38,189
Review Start and the empty End before
continuing.

12
00:00:38,450 --> 00:00:43,079
The initial Narrative explains that this
is a software record-entry exercise and

13
00:00:43,079 --> 00:00:47,269
that no physical training, attendance or
operational response occurred.

14
00:00:47,849 --> 00:00:50,650
Write enough context for someone reading
the record later.

15
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:55,180
Below it, Participants provides a person
selector, an optional

16
00:00:55,180 --> 00:00:56,750
assigned unit and a role.

17
00:00:57,299 --> 00:00:59,219
We leave the participant unselected.

18
00:00:59,699 --> 00:01:03,379
A blank chooser is not an attendance
entry, and adding a name would

19
00:01:03,379 --> 00:01:04,910
make a different factual claim.

20
00:01:05,350 --> 00:01:08,029
The exercise therefore keeps this section
empty.

21
00:01:08,189 --> 00:01:13,370
The Units section offers a checkbox for
each unit and columns for Dispatched, En

22
00:01:13,370 --> 00:01:16,809
route, On scene, Released and In quarters.

23
00:01:17,269 --> 00:01:19,779
These fields belong to the record’s
response data.

24
00:01:20,170 --> 00:01:23,229
They are not instructions to change a
unit’s live status.

25
00:01:23,630 --> 00:01:26,889
We leave all unit selections and time
entries untouched

26
00:01:26,940 --> 00:01:28,420
because no response happened.

27
00:01:28,860 --> 00:01:32,180
Review the displayed rows and preserve
those empty selections

28
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:35,950
rather than entering invented times to
make a record appear complete.

29
00:01:36,109 --> 00:01:40,360
At the bottom, no file is selected and the
attestation is unchecked.

30
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:44,359
Choose Save draft to retain an editable
record for review.

31
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:48,380
The separate Save and finalize control is
visible, but this

32
00:01:48,380 --> 00:01:50,019
first step saves the draft.

33
00:01:50,479 --> 00:01:56,940
The fresh View Record page shows Training
in Draft, with reference D, Y, X, S,

34
00:01:57,129 --> 00:01:59,920
Nine, Three and no final record number.

35
00:02:00,299 --> 00:02:02,299
Author and Owner are Wallace Boden.

36
00:02:02,670 --> 00:02:06,909
Station and group resolved to Command
after the initial field was left blank.

37
00:02:07,299 --> 00:02:10,910
Read the returned group and keep the
actual reference before proceeding.

38
00:02:11,149 --> 00:02:14,429
Use Edit on this saved draft to revise its
content.

39
00:02:14,889 --> 00:02:19,060
Continue with the existing record rather
than opening another New Record form.

40
00:02:19,580 --> 00:02:23,750
The revised Narrative states that this is
software record entry, no physical

41
00:02:23,750 --> 00:02:28,829
training or operational response occurred,
and no personnel or unit times were added.

42
00:02:29,329 --> 00:02:32,829
Course, code and Location remain the
exercise values.

43
00:02:33,370 --> 00:02:37,400
Read the complete text in the visible
editor after typing; the content

44
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:41,240
on screen must match the intended
statement before you rely on it.

45
00:02:41,389 --> 00:02:45,030
A fresh View Record readback now contains
the corrected narrative on

46
00:02:45,030 --> 00:02:47,280
the same draft and the same reference.

47
00:02:47,709 --> 00:02:50,259
It remains Draft, with no final number.

48
00:02:50,719 --> 00:02:54,200
Checking a separate readback is valuable
even when a draft supports

49
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:58,550
automatic saving: text visible in an
editor is not sufficient evidence

50
00:02:58,550 --> 00:03:00,370
that the intended version is stored.

51
00:03:00,749 --> 00:03:04,269
If the result is unexpected, inspect this
existing draft

52
00:03:04,269 --> 00:03:06,189
before repeating a create action.

53
00:03:06,610 --> 00:03:10,390
Here the software-only revision is present
and ready for the next step.

54
00:03:10,710 --> 00:03:13,790
In Attachments, choose the public software
handout and

55
00:03:13,790 --> 00:03:17,770
select Unrestricted operational content in
File classification.

56
00:03:18,170 --> 00:03:22,570
Use this classification because the file
contains only the fictional exercise.

57
00:03:22,890 --> 00:03:24,759
Then use Add attachment.

58
00:03:25,190 --> 00:03:28,890
After a fresh readback,
academy-record-entry-handout dot

59
00:03:28,890 --> 00:03:32,769
text appears in the attachment list with
three hundred fifty-eight bytes.

60
00:03:33,099 --> 00:03:36,459
The upload selector has reset to
Restricted for another file.

61
00:03:36,799 --> 00:03:39,760
That reset does not describe the file
already stored.

62
00:03:40,099 --> 00:03:43,849
We will check the stored classification in
the finalized revision, rather than

63
00:03:43,849 --> 00:03:45,869
inferring it from the new-upload control.

64
00:03:46,139 --> 00:03:48,980
Read the narrative and stored handout
before signing.

65
00:03:49,439 --> 00:03:52,690
The attestation says the record is
accurate and complete to

66
00:03:52,690 --> 00:03:53,969
the best of your knowledge.

67
00:03:54,359 --> 00:03:59,159
In this exercise, accuracy means
accurately documenting a software practice

68
00:03:59,199 --> 00:04:01,439
with no physical training or response.

69
00:04:01,869 --> 00:04:04,409
Check the attestation and use Finalize.

70
00:04:04,879 --> 00:04:09,440
The fresh result shows Finalized and the
actual issued number, T R N,

71
00:04:09,659 --> 00:04:12,170
Twenty Twenty-Six, Zero Zero Zero Two.

72
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:14,260
The draft reference remains.

73
00:04:14,690 --> 00:04:18,879
Amend is now available for later
corrections; a finalized revision

74
00:04:18,879 --> 00:04:22,519
is preserved rather than overwritten by
ordinary draft editing.

75
00:04:22,779 --> 00:04:27,169
History now contains revision one,
transition Finalized, with Wallace

76
00:04:27,169 --> 00:04:28,730
Boden and the recorded date.

77
00:04:29,120 --> 00:04:30,580
The handout remains listed.

78
00:04:31,070 --> 00:04:35,470
Use View on that history row to inspect
what this first signature preserved.

79
00:04:35,909 --> 00:04:39,420
The resulting HTML page identifies the
complete department

80
00:04:39,449 --> 00:04:41,090
record and revision one.

81
00:04:41,519 --> 00:04:45,869
Its Details section contains the Course,
Course Code, Location and

82
00:04:45,869 --> 00:04:47,830
exact software-only Narrative.

83
00:04:48,209 --> 00:04:51,490
This gives a specific version to review,
rather than relying

84
00:04:51,539 --> 00:04:53,310
only on the latest record header.

85
00:04:53,499 --> 00:04:57,800
The same revision lists the handout’s
filename, byte size, checksum

86
00:04:57,840 --> 00:04:59,809
and Unrestricted classification.

87
00:05:00,229 --> 00:05:03,259
This is the stored attachment metadata,
separate from the

88
00:05:03,259 --> 00:05:04,910
reset chooser we saw earlier.

89
00:05:05,269 --> 00:05:07,809
The checksum matches the retained public
handout.

90
00:05:08,419 --> 00:05:13,040
Farther down, Draft Reference, External Id
and Record Number connect the

91
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:15,290
original draft to the finalized record.

92
00:05:15,649 --> 00:05:19,390
Keep those identifiers together when
reviewing an exercise or resolving a

93
00:05:19,390 --> 00:05:21,550
question about which version was signed.

94
00:05:21,830 --> 00:05:25,819
Returning to Records shows the new
finalized Training row alongside

95
00:05:25,819 --> 00:05:29,299
the earlier Work draft and older finalized
Training example.

96
00:05:29,850 --> 00:05:34,739
Read the number, type, state and summary
together to choose the intended row.

97
00:05:35,299 --> 00:05:40,469
The original Work draft remains D, Four
Six Nine K P; the older Training

98
00:05:40,469 --> 00:05:42,860
record ends Zero Zero Zero One.

99
00:05:43,450 --> 00:05:47,010
This exercise created and finalized only
its own entry.

100
00:05:47,460 --> 00:05:51,059
Leave the other records untouched, and use
the actual issued number

101
00:05:51,059 --> 00:05:52,669
when finding this example again.

102
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:56,619
Chicago Fire can use this pattern for a
software orientation record.

103
00:05:57,050 --> 00:06:01,159
Community care and mental-health teams can
document an operational software

104
00:06:01,159 --> 00:06:03,310
exercise without client histories.

105
00:06:03,709 --> 00:06:06,629
Search-and-rescue teams can keep practice
documentation

106
00:06:06,629 --> 00:06:08,529
distinct from a mission response.

107
00:06:08,910 --> 00:06:13,080
Security teams can retain a fictional
platform exercise; wildland

108
00:06:13,080 --> 00:06:17,570
coordinators can document seasonal
software familiarization; industrial

109
00:06:17,570 --> 00:06:21,050
teams can preserve a system-training
example and public handout.

110
00:06:21,480 --> 00:06:26,489
Across all six settings, use accurate
narrative, actual participants and times

111
00:06:26,630 --> 00:06:30,950
only when they occurred, a verified
attachment, and the saved revision that

112
00:06:30,950 --> 00:06:32,800
supports the statement being reviewed.

